<p>With the days growing shorter and work being what it is, I find that once again I don&rsquo;t even leave the apartment — and when I do, it&rsquo;s usually for a short errand like grocery shopping or returning something to Amazon at the local Staples.</p>
<p>Work fills up my time, especially now with less daylight, but that&rsquo;s only half the story. My energy is drained more regularly and thoroughly as I spend more of my <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spoon_theory">spoons</a> there.</p>
<p>Fortunately, I believe that at this point in time the downward spiral has slowed down. I am able to select points in my day where I take care of myself on a regular basis. I manage to sleep more overall. I exercise, though briefly, a couple of times a week (OK, twice, and it&rsquo;s still a struggle, but hey, better than nothing). I go for short walks to get some sun while it&rsquo;s still out. I see a therapist again to keep an eye on everything, and I managed to get a hold of ADHD meds (finally) if I need them.</p>
<p>And yet, or perhaps because of all of this, I find that I&rsquo;m thirsty to be more creative. To do more with my own projects — be it this blog, Emacs geekery, or discovering and playing new video games (rather than playing the same old ones over and over). Hell, I&rsquo;m <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/11/01/old-mans-war-by-john.html">even enjoying a new book</a> I&rsquo;m mostly done with at this point.</p>
<p>Many of my co-workers have kids. As a matter of fact, most of them have kids who have already left home. I find myself wondering how they did it. I can&rsquo;t imagine the sacrifices that had to be made. For me, it would probably mean switching jobs or choosing which people I can&rsquo;t spend time with. While I would never be monogamous, I wouldn&rsquo;t be able to have as many close people to me as I do now. There&rsquo;s simply not enough time in the day, and days in the week.</p>
<p>I realized that if I had kids, they would take priority, leaving me no time to be who I am today. I wouldn&rsquo;t be me  — and I like the person I am.</p>
<p>It&rsquo;s not just me either. I find that my close friends, those in my polyamorous circles, are making the same choices. Only a few days ago, a third one made the choice to undergo a hysterectomy. It&rsquo;s not just a pattern: it&rsquo;s a <em>commitment</em> that goes beyond my simpler choice to remain childfree.</p>
<p>As we grow older, our choices become more of a one-way street. This sounds negative, but it&rsquo;s not inherently bad. Going forward without looking back means no second-guessing, and moving forward at full speed toward what you believe is right for you. It lets you plan ahead on principles you believe in. It means you trust yourself.</p>
